# Test Plan – Guru99 Bank

**Application URL:** https://demo.guru99.com/
**Document Version:** 1.0
**Date:** September 21, 2026
**Prepared By:** QA Team

---

## 1. Introduction

### 1.1 Purpose
This document defines the test strategy, scope, resources, and schedule for testing the Guru99 Bank web application — a demo banking system used for practicing manual and automated testing. It covers functional testing of core banking operations including customer management, account management, and transaction processing.

### 1.2 Application Overview
Guru99 Bank is a sample banking application with two primary user roles:
- **Manager** – manages customers and accounts (add/edit/delete customers, add/delete accounts, view balance)
- **Customer** – performs banking transactions (deposit, withdraw, fund transfer, view statements)

---

## 2. Objectives

- Verify that all functional modules of Guru99 Bank work as per expected business rules.
- Validate login authentication for both Manager and Customer roles.
- Ensure data integrity during CRUD operations on customer and account records.
- Validate transactional operations (deposit, withdrawal, fund transfer) update balances correctly.
- Identify and log defects to ensure a stable, reliable application.

---

## 3. Scope

### 3.1 In Scope
| Module | Features to be Tested |
|---|---|
| Manager Login | Login with valid/invalid credentials |
| Customer Management | Add Customer, Edit Customer, Delete Customer, Search Customer |
| Account Management | Add Account, Delete Account |
| Customer Login | Login with valid/invalid credentials, login by selecting customer |
| Transactions | Deposit, Withdraw, Fund Transfer |
| Account Info | Balance Enquiry, Mini Statement, Change Password |
| Navigation | Menu links, logout functionality |

### 3.2 Out of Scope
- Performance/load testing
- Security penetration testing
- Mobile responsiveness testing
- Backend database validation
- Third-party payment gateway integration (not applicable to demo site)

---

## 4. Test Strategy

### 4.1 Types of Testing
- **Functional Testing** – Validate each feature against expected behavior
- **UI Testing** – Verify layout, field labels, error messages
- **Negative Testing** – Invalid inputs, boundary values, empty fields
- **Regression Testing** – Re-test after defect fixes
- **Cross-browser Testing** – Chrome, Firefox, Edge (basic compatibility check)

### 4.2 Test Levels
- System Testing (primary focus, as this is a black-box demo app)
- User Acceptance Testing (UAT) – optional, based on stakeholder need

### 4.3 Test Approach
All testing will be performed manually. Testers will execute test cases directly in the browser, recording actual results, pass/fail status, and screenshots (where relevant) for each test step. No automation tooling is used in this cycle.

---

## 5. Test Environment

| Item | Details |
|---|---|
| Application URL | https://demo.guru99.com/ |
| Browsers | Chrome (latest), Firefox (latest), Edge (latest) |
| OS | Windows 10/11, macOS |
| Test Data | Dynamically created customers (no fixed accounts; created via Manager module) |
| Tools | Browser DevTools (for element inspection/debugging only); Excel/Sheet or test management tool for logging results |
| Testing Method | 100% Manual |

---

## 6. Entry and Exit Criteria

### 6.1 Entry Criteria
- Test plan reviewed and approved
- Test cases written and reviewed
- Application is accessible and stable at the test URL
- Test data preparation strategy defined

### 6.2 Exit Criteria
- All planned test cases executed
- No open Critical/High severity defects
- Test summary report shared with stakeholders
- 95%+ pass rate on core functional test cases

---

## 7. Test Scenarios (High-Level)

| ID | Scenario | Priority |
|---|---|---|
| TS01 | Verify Manager login with valid/invalid credentials | High |
| TS02 | Verify a new customer can be added successfully | High |
| TS03 | Verify mandatory field validations on Add Customer form | High |
| TS04 | Verify a new account can be opened for an existing customer | High |
| TS05 | Verify customer search by name/account number | Medium |
| TS06 | Verify customer details can be edited | Medium |
| TS07 | Verify a customer can be deleted | Medium |
| TS08 | Verify deleting a customer removes associated accounts | High |
| TS09 | Verify Customer login by selecting account number | High |
| TS10 | Verify deposit updates account balance correctly | High |
| TS11 | Verify withdrawal fails when amount exceeds balance | High |
| TS12 | Verify withdrawal updates balance correctly for valid amount | High |
| TS13 | Verify fund transfer between two valid accounts | High |
| TS14 | Verify fund transfer fails for invalid/nonexistent account | High |
| TS15 | Verify balance enquiry displays correct current balance | Medium |
| TS16 | Verify mini statement displays recent transactions | Low |
| TS17 | Verify logout functionality from Manager and Customer modules | Medium |
| TS18 | Verify negative/boundary inputs (e.g., negative deposit amount) are rejected | High |

*(Detailed step-by-step test cases with expected results should be maintained in a separate Test Case document/sheet.)*

---

## 8. Roles and Responsibilities

| Role | Responsibility |
|---|---|
| Test Lead | Test planning, review, status reporting |
| QA Engineer(s) | Test case design, manual execution, defect logging |

---

## 9. Deliverables

- Test Plan (this document)
- Test Case document
- Defect/Bug reports
- Test Execution/Summary Report

---

## 10. Risks and Mitigation

| Risk | Mitigation |
|---|---|
| Demo site data resets or is shared publicly (other testers may alter/delete data) | Create fresh test data per session; avoid dependency on fixed pre-existing accounts |
| Site downtime/instability (public demo environment) | Retest during off-peak hours; log environment-related blockers separately |
| No password recovery/email validation possible on live demo | Mark such flows as out-of-scope or test with placeholder assumptions |

---

## 11. Assumptions

- The application is a publicly available demo/sandbox environment; no real financial data is involved.
- Test data (customers/accounts) created during testing may be visible to other users of the shared demo site.
- No dedicated staging/QA environment exists separate from the public demo.
